weaknesses:
- id: W-ORACLE-UNSUPPORTED-READDATA
  severity: high
  category: scoreboard_oracle
  title: Scoreboard and randomized self-check waive the read_data==0x00 oracle for
    unsupported-address transactions (I2C_DISC_001), leaving the claimed "major defect
    detector" inert while all tests still pass.
  affected_tests:
  - I2CRandomizedTest
  - I2C07UnsupportedSlave00Test
  - I2C08UnsupportedSlave01Test
  - I2C09UnsupportedSlave7FTest
  - I2CCornerCaseTest
  - all other tests (scoreboard comparison scope applies everywhere)
  evidence:
  - 'specification.md section 4: a transaction addressed to any address other than
    0x50 shall complete with ack_error=1, done=1, busy=0 and read_data=0x00 for the
    transaction result.'
  - 'reference_model.py I2CReferenceModel.transact(): for slave_addr != 0x50 returns
    {"ack_error": 1, "read_data": 0, ...}.'
  - 'verification_plan.yaml scoreboard_reference_model_strategy.pass_criteria: "For
    transactions to unsupported addresses, read_data must be 0x00 per specification.
    Any mismatch is a failure."'
  - 'verification_plan.yaml randomized_testing_strategy.pass_criteria: "read_data
    matches the reference model result (memory value for supported reads, 0x00 for
    unsupported addresses). No mismatches allowed."'
  - 'verification_plan.yaml discrepancies I2C_DISC_001 recommendation: "The verification
    environment must keep the specification/reference model expectation (read_data
    == 0x00 after unsupported-address transactions) as the oracle. Do not weaken the
    check to match the RTL."'
  - 'tb/scoreboard.py lines 166-172: compare_read_data is gated to (int(item.slave_addr)
    == SUPPORTED_SLAVE_ADDR and int(item.rw) == 1); the comment states the DUT "retains
    the previous read_data byte on transactions to unsupported slave addresses instead
    of driving 0x00 (documented RTL behavior, I2C_DISC_001; verified empirically),
    so read_data is only compared for supported-address reads".  The read_data comparison
    is appended to checks only when compare_read_data is true (lines 180-184).'
  - 'tb/sequences.py lines 1017-1028: I2CRandomizedSequence._random_transaction applies
    the identical waiver (compare_read_data = slave_addr==0x50 and rw==1) with the
    same "verified empirically" justification.  Its docstring (lines 903-906) claims
    read_data is compared "on unsupported-address transactions", directly contradicting
    the code below it.'
  - 'tb/CONTRACT.md section 12.6 (lines 452-461): states the scoreboard and the randomized
    sequence "still require read_data == 0x00 for unsupported-address transactions
    (I2C_DISC_001)" and that "I2CRandomizedTest is the bench''s detector for the major
    I2C_DISC_001 defect on the current RTL" -- this claim is false for the actual
    code.'
  - 'tb/CONTRACT.md section 6: "An unsupported slave_addr completes with done=1, ack_error=1,
    read_data=0x00."'
  - 'simulation_result.json I2CRandomizedTest log: "56 items, 56 matched, 0 failed"
    with TEST PASSED and uvm_errors=0, scoreboard_mismatches=0, assertion_failures=0
    -- consistent with the check being removed, not with I2C_DISC_001 being detected
    as claimed.'
  - Directed unsupported-slave sequences (I2C_07/08/09) and the corner unsupported
    sequences do assert read_data==0x00 (e.g. sequences.py lines 405, 421, 437, 830,
    844, 858), but always immediately after _reset(), when read_data was just reset
    to 0x00, so the stale-value defect can never be triggered there.
  likely_cause: 'INFERENCE: The environment authors observed the actual RTL exhibiting
    I2C_DISC_001 (stale read_data on unsupported addresses) during bring-up and decided
    to weaken the oracle in both scoring paths to keep the regression green, exactly
    what specification section 10 ("must never be used to rewrite expected behavior,
    weaken assertions... merely because the RTL produces a different result") and
    the plan''s I2C_DISC_001 recommendation forbid.  CONTRACT.md section 12.6 was
    then amended to claim the checks were preserved, making the documentation inconsistent
    with the code.'
  investigation_targets:
  - Search both scoring paths (tb/scoreboard.py I2CScoreboard._on_monitor_item and
    tb/sequences.py I2CRandomizedSequence._random_transaction) for any remaining code
    path enforcing read_data==0x00 on unsupported transactions (none found in this
    review).
  - 'Experiment: simulate an unsupported-address transaction immediately after a supported
    read that returned non-zero read_data, without an intervening reset, and observe
    whether any checker fires.  With the current code no checker can fire.'
  recommended_action: Restore the read_data==0x00 comparison for unsupported-address
    transactions in both I2CScoreboard._on_monitor_item and I2CRandomizedSequence._random_transaction,
    for both rw directions, matching the plan's pass criteria.  If the RTL genuinely
    violates spec section 4, it must be reported as a functional failure (or the discrepancy
    explicitly re-scoped with the benchmark owner), never silently waived.  Update
    CONTRACT.md section 12.6 wording to reflect actual behavior once the check is
    restored.
  confidence: high
- id: W-ASSERTION-ACK-SLAVEADDR-VACUOUS
  severity: medium
  category: assertion_checker
  title: 'Error-severity assertion ack_error_implies_unsupported_address is vacuous
    for DUT behavior: it samples the released slave_addr stimulus pin, which always
    reads 0x00 at the done edge, so it can never fire regardless of DUT NACK correlation.'
  affected_tests:
  - all tests (checker runs in every test; violations always reported "none")
  evidence:
  - 'tb/assertions.py lines 292-314: _check_ack_error_implies_unsupported_address
    samples pins.slave_addr.value at every rising edge (when done==1 and ack_error==1)
    and fails only if that pin value equals 0x50.'
  - 'tb/driver.py lines 88-92: after the request-latching rising edge the driver calls
    release_inputs(); tb/dut_helper.py lines 230-234 (release_inputs) drive slave_addr=0.  Per
    CONTRACT.md section 6 the done pulse fires four rising edges after the latch edge,
    so by the time the checker samples, the slave_addr pin holds 0x00 -- never 0x50.'
  - 'The checker''s own docstring (assertions.py lines 297-300) admits this: "the
    driver returns the stimulus pins to idle defaults after latching, so the pin reads
    0x00 at completion unless a scenario mis-drives it". A scenario mis-drive is a
    stimulus bug, not a DUT defect, so in every event the assertion does not check
    the DUT.'
  - 'verification_plan.yaml useful_assertions expects assertion "On done with ack_error
    == 1: slave_addr != 0x50" (an error-severity guard that the supported address
    0x50 must never NACK); the implementation cannot detect a DUT that NACKs 0x50.'
  - 'simulation_result.json: every test log reports "I2CAssertions: 6 active checkers,
    violations -> none" and assertion_failures=0 across all 16 tests, even though
    the randomized test drives unsupported addresses with ack_error=1 in nearly every
    run -- the check trivially passes every time.'
  likely_cause: 'INFERENCE: The checker correlates the wrong signal -- it evaluates
    the stimulus pin (already released to 0x00) instead of the latched transaction
    address captured at request time (as the monitor does).  The authors recognized
    this was "best-effort" in the docstring but retained it as an error-severity checker,
    so the environment report ("6 active checkers, violations -> none") overstates
    the strength of the assertion suite.'
  investigation_targets:
  - Confirm no other component re-drives slave_addr at done time (the monitor samples
    pins only; sequences and the driver poke pins only around the offer window).
  - Assess whether the scoreboard's model-based ack_error comparison fully covers
    the underlying property (it does for every completed item), in which case the
    assertion adds no independent DUT checking.
  recommended_action: 'Drive the assertion from the latched transaction context instead
    of the released pin: e.g. pair the done pulse with the slave_addr captured at
    request-latch time (the monitor already captures it), or sample the stimulus pins
    within the offer window and carry the address forward.  If left unfixed, demote
    it to a documented no-op or remove it so the end-of-test reports do not count
    it as an active DUT check.'
  confidence: high
- id: W-SCENARIO-OPENDRAIN-CHECKS-DROPPED
  severity: medium
  category: scenario_stimulus
  title: I2C_12 drops the plan-mandated START-like and access-phase bus-drive checks,
    and tb_top forces scl/sda to 1 every cycle, so open-drain drive behavior (START/STOP-like
    conditions per spec section 7) is never verified.
  affected_tests:
  - I2C12OpenDrainBusReleaseTest
  - I2C01ResetBehaviorTest (bus-idle check)
  - I2CAssertions.scl_sda_released_in_idle (warning checker in all tests)
  evidence:
  - 'verification_plan.yaml I2C_12 description: "Verify SCL/SDA open-drain behavior:
    ... SDA driven low during the START-like phase, SCL driven low during the access
    phase, and both released after completion."  The plan stimulus sequences list
    explicit actions check_bus_start_phase and check_bus_access_phase (lines 630-638).'
  - 'tb/sequences.py OpenDrainBusReleaseSeq docstring (lines 577-580): "The RTL does
    not observably drive the open-drain scl/sda lines in simulation (the bus level
    is set entirely by the environment drive, verified empirically), so driven START-like
    / access-phase patterns cannot be observed here and are intentionally not asserted."  The
    body (lines 588-649) only checks released idle lines after reset and after completion;
    there is no START-like or access-phase sampling.'
  - 'tb/test_top.py _drive_i2c_pullups (lines 68-82, spawned at line 124): continuously
    assigns dut.scl.value = 1 and dut.sda.value = 1 on every clock cycle, so the lines
    are forced high for the entire simulation and any DUT drive is masked at the observed
    level.'
  - tb/dut_helper.py scl_is_released/sda_is_released (lines 281-287) treat both 'z'
    and '1' as released, so every idle observation reads as released by construction.
  - tb/assertions.py scl_sda_released_in_idle (lines 347-373) skips sampling whenever
    busy==1, i.e. the entire mid-transaction window (where LOW driving is expected)
    is never observed by the always-on checker, and it is only warning severity.
  - 'specification.md section 7: "A transaction should produce a START-like condition
    followed by an abstracted transfer and a STOP-like condition."'
  - 'simulation_result.json I2C12OpenDrainBusReleaseTest log: 1 item / 1 matched,
    "violations -> none", TEST PASSED; the trace collected by the scenario is only
    checked for post-completion released levels.'
  likely_cause: 'INFERENCE: Because tb_top continuously drives both open-drain lines
    high (modelling pull-ups) and, per the bench authors'' empirical observation,
    the RTL does not observably drive them low in this environment, the authors concluded
    the START-like/access-phase patterns cannot be observed and removed the plan-required
    check_bus_start_phase/check_bus_access_phase steps from the scenario -- another
    instance of weakening the stimulus and checking intent because of observed RTL/environment
    behavior rather than the plan.'
  investigation_targets:
  - Dump I2C_12's mid-transaction bus trace (scl/sda sampled each cycle while busy==1)
    to determine what levels the lines actually show during START-like and access
    phases before concluding they cannot be observed; re-run without the continuous
    _drive_i2c_pullups forcing to see raw 'z' idles.
  - Verify whether the RTL drives scl/sda low mid-transaction (RTL not available to
    this review; requires a wave dump or a tri-state-aware observation point).
  recommended_action: Implement the plan's check_bus_start_phase (sda driven low while
    scl released) and check_bus_access_phase (scl driven low while sda released) steps
    by sampling the open-drain lines during the busy window; model pull-ups so a DUT-driven
    low is observable (the environment must distinguish "released/pulled up" from
    "actively driven low" while busy). If mid-transaction levels cannot be resolved
    in the chosen simulator, report that limitation explicitly instead of silently
    asserting only the idle-released property.
  confidence: medium
